   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.4. Increased capacity of constituents to provide adequate labour protection to workers in diverse forms of work arrangements, including on digital labour platforms, and in informal employment</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">In December 2021, the Ministry of Labour and other social partners validated the first draft of the gender-responsive diagnoses of 1) care economy workers to facilitate transition to formality; and 2) home-based women workers and other informal economy workers in global supply chains and their linkages with subcontractors or MSEs in the informal economy to use as evidence on decent work deficits of informal economy workers (in line with C177). 
The PROTECT Union and Domestic Workers Union conducted a rapid assessment of the impact of COVID-19 on 600 domestic workers and 50 employers in 2020. A tripartite forum validated the results and recommendations.
The unions also provided personal protective equipment and food aid in response to COVID-19. PROTECT Union conducted training programmes on social security (March 14 and April 4th 2021) resulting in 100 domestic workers registering for social security schemes, improving worker protection during the pandemic. Campaign by PROTECT Union and Domestic Workers Union on rights of domestic workers, including on C189 (using leaflets, meetings, social media and video advocacy) promoting C189 and the International Domestic Workers Day lead to increased enrolment of membership of both Unions. The two technical meetings on domestic work and C189 held on 18 June 2021, and 17 August 2021 by PROTECT and the Centre for Working Women reached approximately 2000 domestic workers and other stakeholders. These contributed to the Minister of Labour&apos;s commitment to extend minimum wage and social security protection to domestic workers. ILO technical assistance for the study on &#x2018;Care Economy in Sri Lanka&apos;s Future of Work&apos; to provide evidence base and policy recommendations.
ILO technical assistance for evidence-base on decent work deficits of home-based and informal economy workers as well as law and policy review conducted to support the development of a national policy with support from the Government of Japan. 
Capacity building of trade unions and membership based organizations in increasing representation, leadership and participation in social dialogue of home-based and informal workers in lower tiers of the supply chains, including on piece rate wages and Occupational Safety and Health.
ILO provided support on domestic workers to the Workers&apos; Organisation with Swedish funding, including a Rapid Assessment on COVID19 impacts on Domestic Workers in 2020. This provided the evidence basis for the social security campaign leading to improved enrolment of domestic workers in social security programmes. 
The ILO provided assistance to the unions to provide food and personal protective equipment for domestic workers affected by COVID19.
The ILO provided assistance to support the awareness-raising campaigns led by the trade unions. 
ILO technical assistance on C189 provided to the Workers&apos; Organisations that was funded by SIDA, included support on its promotion and refinement of messages; The ILO study on decent work deficits in domestic work  was presented to the Government of Sri Lanka among others.</narrative>
   </description>
